# P94 — Red team round 2, wave 1: what key deletion is, legally and in the model.
# RT2 consistency M1 (verified 31 Oct against the EDPB PDF): Guidelines 02/2025 v2.0 do NOT say key deletion
#   serves erasure. Para 51: key deletion makes encrypted data unintelligible "at least until the algorithm is
#   broken", and "encrypted personal data is still personal data"; para 50: technical impossibility is no
#   excuse; para 125: erasure means "erased or rendered anonymous". Restated everywhere.
# RT2 examiner M-2: the model cannot distinguish key destruction from deletion; crypto is for copies.
# M-6: per-record keys are the simplest construction, not required (Cachin et al., 2013).
# M-7: verifiability overclaim trimmed (§6.3, §7.2). m-3 end-of-life residue; never needs "rewriting".
# m-9 components the mechanism actually uses; m-10 AES refusal vs cross-key check; m-11 substrate claim.
# RT2 consistency M2 (Annex 7 context) fixed where it shares a paragraph (§2.4, §6.2).
