 dict(id='P94-07', file='Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md',
  old=r"""First, the technical components required for a verifiable cryptographic-erasure mechanism for cross-border health audit trails all exist in mature form — trusted execution environments at the OpenNCP layer, zero-knowledge proof systems applied to healthcare, blockchain-anchored consent and audit infrastructures with Norwegian clinical validation, cryptographic accumulators with revocation semantics, and a formally grounded cryptographic-erasure foundation — each reviewed above. Second, none of these works combines these components to address the specific tension between eHDSI-mandated audit retention, GDPR Article 17 erasure rights, and TEHDAS2 Secure Processing Environment deletion in the cross-border European context. Third, the Joint Action drafting the Secure Processing Environment specification itself records that further guidance is needed on how accountability and anonymisation obligations intersect (TEHDAS2, 2026b, Annex 7, p. 96). The contribution of this thesis is positioned at that named gap.""",
  new=r"""First, the technical components on which a verifiable cryptographic-erasure mechanism for cross-border health audit trails can draw all exist in mature form: per-record envelope encryption under a key hierarchy, hiding commitments, tamper-evident Merkle logs, cryptographic erase as recognised by NIST, and cryptographic accumulators with revocation semantics, together with options this mechanism does not use, namely trusted execution environments at the OpenNCP layer, zero-knowledge proof systems applied to healthcare, and blockchain-anchored consent and audit infrastructures, each reviewed above. Second, none of these works combines them to address the specific tension between long cross-border audit-retention floors, GDPR Article 17 erasure rights, and TEHDAS2 Secure Processing Environment deletion in the cross-border European context. Third, the Joint Action drafting the Secure Processing Environment specification asks operators to balance deletion against applicable legal obligations without saying how (TEHDAS2, 2026b, Table 5.2, OPR-6, p. 43), and its expert commentary, writing of the project logbooks researchers keep, notes open questions about how accountability and anonymisation obligations intersect (TEHDAS2, 2026b, Annex 7, p. 96). The contribution of this thesis is positioned at the first of these, the balance the specification asks for and does not define.""",
  why="m-9 components actually used; RT2-M3 'eHDSI-mandated'; RT2-M2 Annex 7 in context, gap rests on OPR-6"),
