 dict(id='P94-12', file='Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md',
  old=r"""Per-record rather than per-subject or per-dataset granularity is required because the invalidation events of Proposition 2 occur at record granularity: a permit expiry invalidates the records of one permit, while an erasure request invalidates the records relating to one data subject, and these sets intersect without nesting.""",
  new=r"""Per-record granularity is the simplest construction that meets the invalidation events of Proposition 2, which occur at record granularity: a permit expiry invalidates the records of one permit, while an erasure request invalidates the records relating to one data subject, and these sets intersect without nesting, so no single per-subject or per-dataset key serves both. It is not the only one. A record encrypted so that a key share for its subject and a key share for its permit are each needed to read it becomes unreadable when either share is destroyed, with a key store that grows with subjects and permits rather than with records; policy-based secure deletion generalises the idea (Cachin, Haralambiev, Hsiao, & Sorniotti, 2013). That construction is not implemented here.""",
  why="M-6: per-record keys simplest, not required; name the alternative"),
 dict(id='P94-13', file='Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md',
  old=r"""Key destruction remains the operative erasure for copies the store cannot delete at once, such as replicas and backups: they become unreadable at $t^{I}(a)$ and leave by ordinary rotation. The specification for Secure Processing Environments accepts exactly this:""",
  new=r"""Key destruction remains the operative means for copies the store cannot delete at once, such as replicas and backups: they become unreadable at $t^{I}(a)$ and leave by ordinary rotation. Until they leave, the Board holds that encrypted data of this kind are still personal data (European Data Protection Board, 2026b), so for those copies key destruction secures unintelligibility, not erasure. Nothing in the TRF Model distinguishes key destruction from deletion of the payload: both set $\alpha$ to zero, and the propositions hold for either. The case for keys rests on these copies, which the model does not represent and which the specification for Secure Processing Environments expressly reaches:""",
  why="M-2 + RT2-M1: model is indifferent between key destruction and deletion; crypto is for copies"),
