 dict(id='P94-16', file='Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md',
  old=r"""*Why the mechanism is nonetheless defensible.* The applicable legal test is not anonymisation but erasure. The European Data Protection Board's Guidelines 02/2025 accept that where physical deletion is not technically possible, deleting the decryption key can render data unintelligible and so serve the erasure obligation. They maintain at the same time that encrypted and hashed data may remain personal data, and that the unintelligibility holds at least until the algorithm is broken (European Data Protection Board, 2026b). The requirement that the controller document the threat model under which the data are considered unrecoverable is not the Board's formulation. It is Aikebaier's (2026) interpretation, which connects a Destruction-IND security notion to the Board's criterion,""",
  new=r"""*Why the mechanism is nonetheless defensible.* The applicable legal test is not anonymisation but erasure. The mechanism meets it for the store itself by deletion: at $t^{I}(a)$ the payload's ciphertext, key and salt are deleted (Section 4.8). Key destruction is relied on only for copies that cannot be deleted at once, and for those the European Data Protection Board's Guidelines 02/2025 do not settle the question. They state that upon deletion of the decryption key the encrypted data will be unintelligible, at least until the algorithm is broken, but hold in the same paragraph that encrypted personal data is still personal data, and require that on an erasure request the data be erased or rendered anonymous (European Data Protection Board, 2026b). Such copies are therefore a bounded residue, unreadable from $t^{I}(a)$ and personal data until they leave by rotation, and are stated as such. The requirement that the controller document the threat model under which the data are considered unrecoverable is not the Board's formulation. It is Aikebaier's (2026) interpretation, which connects a Destruction-IND security notion to the Board's description of unintelligibility,""",
  why="RT2-M1: §4.9 rests defensibility on deletion; key-destroyed copies a stated residue"),
